Q: .net MAUI CollectionView inside of ScrollView A: <p>I get why you try to put a <code>CollectionView</code> inside a <code>ScrollView</code>. However, embedded scrolls are generally to be avoided.</p>
<p>In your case, you don't need a ScrollView. You can exclusively use a collection view.
Then, just put all the content that is on top of your collection view as a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/maui/user-interface/controls/collectionview/layout?view=net-maui-9.0#headers-and-footers" rel="nofollow noreferrer">header</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;CollectionView ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding SubActivities}&quot; MinimumHeightRequest=&quot;300&quot;&gt;
    &lt;CollectionView.Header&gt;
        &lt;Grid x:Name=&quot;ContentContainer&quot; Padding=&quot;0,10,0,20&quot; RowDefinition=&quot;*,Auto&quot;&gt;
    &lt;!--  overview Frame  --&gt;
            &lt;Border HeightRequest=&quot;-1&quot; Style=&quot;{StaticResource BorderContainerStyle}&quot;&gt;
        Container with general information
    &lt;/Border&gt;
    &lt;Grid&gt;
    &lt;/CollectionView.Header&gt;
    &lt;CollectionView.ItemsLayout&gt;
        &lt;GridItemsLayout
            Orientation=&quot;Vertical&quot;
            Span=&quot;1&quot;
            VerticalItemSpacing=&quot;5&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemsLayout&gt;
    &lt;CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
        &lt;DataTemplate x:DataType=&quot;activityDetail:SubActivityViewModel&quot;&gt;
            &lt;StackLayout Spacing=&quot;5&quot;&gt;
                &lt;Border IsVisible=&quot;{Binding ShowSubActivity}&quot; Style=&quot;{StaticResource BorderContainerStyle}&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;Border.GestureRecognizers&gt;
                        &lt;TapGestureRecognizer
                            Command=&quot;{Binding Path=ShowAttachments, Source={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type activityDetail:ActivityDetailViewModel}}}&quot;
                            CommandParameter=&quot;{Binding .}&quot;
                            NumberOfTapsRequired=&quot;2&quot; /&gt;
                    &lt;/Border.GestureRecognizers&gt;
                    &lt;templates:DefectAttachmentTemplate BindingContext=&quot;{Binding}&quot; /&gt;
                &lt;/Border&gt;
                &lt;CollectionView ItemsSource=&quot;{Binding VisibleAttachments}&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;CollectionView.ItemsLayout&gt;
                        &lt;GridItemsLayout
                            Orientation=&quot;Vertical&quot;
                            Span=&quot;{Binding GridColumns}&quot;
                            VerticalItemSpacing=&quot;5&quot; /&gt;
                    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemsLayout&gt;
                    &lt;CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
                        &lt;DataTemplate x:DataType=&quot;activityDetail:AttachmentViewModel&quot;&gt;
                            &lt;Border Padding=&quot;0&quot; Style=&quot;{StaticResource BorderContainerStyle}&quot;&gt;
                                &lt;StackLayout&gt;
                                    &lt;templates:ImageControlAttachmentTemplate BindingContext=&quot;{Binding}&quot; /&gt;
                                &lt;/StackLayout&gt;
                            &lt;/Border&gt;
                        &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
                    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
                &lt;/CollectionView&gt;
            &lt;/StackLayout&gt;
        &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
&lt;/CollectionView&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: Material-UI DataGrid Component Refresh with new State Data A: <p>Seems like you mutate state here</p>
<pre><code>this.state.rows.splice(id - 1, 1)
</code></pre>
<p>Try this one</p>
<pre><code>handleDelete() {
    for (let id of this.state.selected) {
        this.props.onDelete(this.hrefs[id]); //delete from database
    }
    this.setState((prevState) =&gt; ({
      ...prevState,
      rows: prevState.rows.filter((row) =&gt; !prevState.selected.includes(row.id)),
      selected: [], 
      nbRender: 2
    }));
}
</code></pre>
